# thermald-minibook-rebased.patch
#
# CHUWI MiniBook X (N100/N150) thermal_daemon customisations, rebased onto
# current upstream master.
#
# UPSTREAM BASE
#   repository : https://github.com/intel/thermal_daemon
#   branch     : master
#   commit     : abfef894d12f2ad057a87da350b72ec190a5f18d
#   date       : 2026-08-14 (committer date, "Merge pull request #593 from intel/thermald_next")
#   version    : thermald 2.5.12-rc1
#
# Apply with:
#   git clone https://github.com/intel/thermal_daemon
#   cd thermal_daemon
#   git checkout abfef894d12f2ad057a87da350b72ec190a5f18d
#   git apply /path/to/thermald-minibook-rebased.patch      # or: patch -p1 < ...
#
# WHAT THIS PATCH ADDS (11 files, 1 of them new)
#   src/thd_minibook_config.h   NEW. All MiniBook tunables in one place:
#                               SEN3 trip 50000 mC, PPCC PL1 8000-17000 mW /
#                               step 500 mW, RAPL PID gains (-1000/-100/-10),
#                               minibook_ec zone names + trips (SoC 80 C,
#                               charger 60 C), TjMax/TCC-offset sysfs paths.
#   configure.ac                Version suffix -> 2.5.12-rc1.minibook1.
#   src/main.cpp                Require --adaptive; abort otherwise. Sets
#                               adaptive_perf_enable before engine creation.
#   src/thd_cdev_rapl.{cpp,h}   enable_pid() + MiniBook PID gains on the RAPL
#                               cdev; control_begin() overridden to a no-op so
#                               the two-trip pattern cannot reset the integral.
#   src/thd_engine.{cpp,h}      New virtual update_power_policies(), called
#                               once per poll interval under the engine lock.
#   src/thd_engine_adaptive.{cpp,h}
#                               PPCC PL1 override when firmware PPCC is locked
#                               (min == max); SEN3 trip cap; install_minibook_ec_zones();
#                               verify_dptf_participants(); verify_rapl_control();
#                               log_tcc_offset(); update_power_policies() applying
#                               GDDV PBAT/PBCT power policies.
#   src/thd_gddv.{cpp,h}        GDDV PBAT/PBCT table parsing (parse_pbat/parse_pbct,
#                               evaluate_pbct_conditions); two extra comparison
#                               operators (ADAPTIVE_GREATER_THAN,
#                               ADAPTIVE_GREATER_OR_EQUAL_2); battery-percentage
#                               (Aggregate_power_percentage) condition evaluation
#                               via upower; activation of the Passive 1 INT3400
#                               UUID when the current UUID is INVALID.
#
# LOCAL HARDENING ADDED BY THIS PATCH -- NOT UPSTREAM CODE
#   parse_pbat() and parse_pbct() are MiniBook-local parsers that read a
#   firmware-supplied blob inside a root daemon. They were modelled on
#   upstream's parse_apat()/parse_apct() before upstream hardened those, and
#   so had opted out of two guards that upstream now relies on. Both are fixed
#   here. Neither exists upstream; do not report them as upstream regressions.
#
#     1. Missing `gddv_cur_buf_len = len;` at function entry.
#        gddv_cur_buf_len is the member every bounds check in get_uint64(),
#        get_string() and get_string_obj() validates against. Because the two
#        PBAT/PBCT parsers never set it, all their reads were validated
#        against a stale length left behind by whichever table was parsed
#        previously. When that earlier table was larger than the current
#        PBAT/PBCT buffer, the bounds checks passed and the parser read past
#        the end of the buffer. This is an out-of-bounds read as root, and it
#        silently defeated upstream commit f3f6875 "Boundry checks for GDDV
#        data vault". Both parsers now set it, matching every upstream parser.
#
#     2. Missing count cap in the PBCT v2 path.
#        parse_pbct() v2 read a condition count straight out of the blob and
#        looped on it unbounded. Mirrors upstream's MAX_APCT_COUNT guard in
#        parse_apct() (thd_gddv.cpp, "Limit max APCT conditions"), using a
#        local MAX_PBCT_COUNT of 128 -- same value, same log wording, same
#        `return THD_ERROR` handling -- because upstream's constant is
#        APCT-specific. The v1 path was already bounded by a fixed loop of 10.
#
#   Neither change alters behaviour on well-formed firmware data.
#
# RELATION TO THE BUNDLED FORK
#   This patch SUPERSEDES the pre-patched source tree bundled at
#   upstream/thermal_daemon/ in this repository. That tree was branched from
#   upstream commit 14bad673097effb29d4d49c1567f92bc1ca13980 (2026-04-28) and
#   therefore predates, among ~80 upstream commits, two security fixes:
#
#     59f223bff55197caffda5dc14f32046f502ef27e  "Sanitize data vault from
#         lib/firmware" (2026-05-04) - the root daemon parses
#         /lib/firmware/intel/dtt/dtt_data_vault_*.bin; require root ownership,
#         reject group/other-writable files and symlinks.
#     f5f5eb8372ab49af150a3e10abdd78cff9527379  "Fix a stale mutex unlock"
#         (2026-05-04) - cthd_engine::user_add_sensor() called
#         thd_engine_mutex.unlock() on an unheld mutex for an empty path.
#
#   Both are present in the base commit this patch applies to (upstream has
#   since hardened gddv_load() further with O_NOFOLLOW, fstat, S_ISREG and a
#   max-size check). Build from upstream master + this patch, not from the
#   bundled tree.
#
# BUILD VERIFICATION
#   The rebased tree was NOT link-built (this rebase was performed on macOS;
#   thermald needs Linux-only headers - linux/input.h, linux/netlink.h,
#   asm/ioctl.h - plus upower-glib and libevdev). Every translation unit the
#   patch touches was verified with `g++ -fsyntax-only -std=c++17 -Wall`
#   against shimmed Linux headers, and passes with zero errors and zero
#   warnings. A native Linux build has not been run; do so before deploying.
#
